\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
There is something curious about Ezekiel 32. It brings in little key bits of the previous several oracles against Egypt. So much so that one might ask what is in Ezekiel 32 that is not in the previous chapters. But generally, when Ezekiel repeats, he adds to our understanding of what he repeats. See the messages of Ezekiel 29, 30, and 31 to see what is being repeated. Most recently, Ezekiel 31 started and ended with the pomp of Pharaoh, his big noise - and its fate. But that fate explicitly generalizes; all trees, that is, all nations, all that drink water, that is, everything dependent, all end up in the depths.\
And now, this first oracle of Ezekiel 32 raises a lament. And so we must use the text and what it contains to help us understand how that lament is being used, how the repetition is being used, how the new features are being used, to communicate the purpose and rhetoric of this oracle.
