\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1502 \u1461 \u1497 \u1502 \u1461 \u1497 \u1492 \u1462 \u1501 
\f1 \cf2  
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1504 \u1463 \u1492 \u1458 \u1512 \u1493 \u1465 \u1514 \u1464 \u1501 
\f1 \cf2  
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1499 \u1463 \u1468 \u1513 \u1462 \u1468 \u1473 \u1502 \u1462 \u1503 
\f1 \cf2  
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1488 \u1493 \u1465 \u1500 \u1460 \u1497 \u1498 \u1456 
\f1 \cf2  \'97 the waters settle, the rivers run smooth as oil. This is genuinely new. Chapters 29-31 indict Pharaoh for a 
\i \cf2 claim
\i0 \cf2  (my Nile, I made it; 29:3) and for pride (heart lifted up; 31:10). Chapter 32:1-16 indicts him for an 
\i \cf2 effect on the shared medium
\i0 \cf2 . Note 
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1504 \u1463 \u1492 \u1458 \u1512 \u1493 \u1465 \u1514 \u1464 \u1501 
\f1 \cf2  \'97 "their rivers," third plural: the fouled waters aren't only Pharaoh's Nile but the nations' waters. The great power's activity degrades the common water, and its removal is not merely death but clarification. That is a different moral register than mortality-of-the-tall-tree in 31.\
The lion/tannim pairing in v. 2 answers chapter 31's "to whom are you like" \'97 degradingly. Chapter 31 asked 
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1488 \u1462 \u1500 \u1470 \u1502 \u1460 \u1497 
\f1 \cf2  
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1491 \u1464 \u1468 \u1502 \u1460 \u1497 \u1514 \u1464 
\f1 \cf2  and answered "the greatest cedar." Here the same root returns: 
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1504 \u1460 \u1491 \u1456 \u1502 \u1461 \u1497 \u1514 \u1464 
\f1 \cf2 . But now the self-image (
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1499 \u1456 \u1468 \u1508 \u1460 \u1497 \u1512 
\f1 \cf2  
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1490 \u1468 \u1493 \u1465 \u1497 \u1460 \u1501 
\f1 \cf2 , the noble predator, king of beasts) is stripped and replaced by the reality (the tannim that only churns mud). Where 31 exalted before felling, 32:2 demeans at the outset.\
And I'd press the verb further: 
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1504 \u1460 \u1491 \u1456 \u1502 \u1461 \u1497 \u1514 \u1464 
\f1 \cf2  is very likely a deliberate pun. It's the exact form of Isaiah 6:5's 
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1504 \u1460 \u1491 \u1456 \u1502 \u1461 \u1497 \u1514 \u1460 \u1497 
\f1 \cf2  \'97 "I am undone/silenced" (
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1491 \u1502 \u1492 
\f1 \cf2  II). In a 
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1511 \u1460 \u1497 \u1504 \u1464 \u1492 
\f1 \cf2 , the genre-fitting reading is not "you likened yourself to a lion" but "you are silenced, O lion of nations! \'97 but you were really the tannim..." The dirge announces the death in its first clause, then corrects the identity in the second. Given your project opened with Isaiah 6, the resonance is worth weighing rather than dismissing; I hold this at moderate confidence, since most translations take 
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1491 \u1502 \u1492 
\f1 \cf2  I here.\
The genuinely new material \'97 the flood of blood (vv. 5-6) and the darkening of the lights (vv. 7-8) \'97 is Exodus redone at cosmic scale, not generic day-of-YHWH imagery. 
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1504 \u1464 \u1514 \u1463 \u1514 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 
\f1 \cf2  
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1495 \u1465 \u1513 \u1462 \u1473 \u1498 \u1456 
\f1 \cf2  
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1506 \u1463 \u1500 \u1470 \u1488 \u1463 \u1512 \u1456 \u1510 \u1456 \u1498 \u1464 
\f1 \cf2  (v. 8) is close to Exodus 10:22's 
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1495 \u1465 \u1513 \u1462 \u1473 \u1498 \u1456 
\f1 \cf2  
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1489 \u1456 \u1468 \u1499 \u1464 \u1500 \u1470 \u1488 \u1462 \u1512 \u1462 \u1509 
\f1 \cf2  
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1502 \u1460 \u1510 \u1456 \u1512 \u1463 \u1497 \u1460 \u1501 
\f1 \cf2  \'97 the ninth plague. And the blood soaking the land answers the first plague, but inverted: the one who turned the Nile churns it no more because his own blood now floods it. Following the Ezekiel 30 and 31 messages' insistence \'97 Torah first, YHWH does again what YHWH did \'97 the escalation isn't Ezekiel reaching for apocalyptic color. It's the plagues generalized: the darkness that once stopped at Egypt's border now dims sun, moon, and stars. The scale is the lament's work: the death of the greatest water-beast is portrayed as a partial un-creation of the Genesis 1 lights.\
The lament performs the death and hands the performance to the nations, which is how the first oracle sets up the second. A 
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1511 \u1460 \u1497 \u1504 \u1464 \u1492 
\f1 \cf2  is sung over a corpse; raising it over a living Pharaoh declares him already dead (as in 19 and 24). But v. 16 insists the chanters are 
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1489 \u1456 \u1468 \u1504 \u1493 \u1465 \u1514 
\f1 \cf2  
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1492 \u1463 \u1490 \u1468 \u1493 \u1465 \u1497 \u1460 \u1501 
\f1 \cf2  \'97 the daughters of the nations. The second oracle (vv. 17-32) then reveals why that matters: those same nations \'97 Asshur, Elam, Meshech-Tubal, Edom, the princes of the north, Sidon \'97 are already in the pit receiving Egypt. So the living nations chant a funeral that is, in substance, their own kind of death sung over the newest arrival. The first oracle's generalization is thus carried not by an explicit "all trees / all who drink water" (that was 31's move) but by the identity of the singers: everyone who sings this song is or will be its subject.\
On your framing \'97 what is in 32 that is not in 29-31 \'97 I'd answer: the concrete water-fouling-as-crime and its clarification-as-remedy; the cosmic/plague darkening; the lion self-image stripped; and the communal chant assigned to the nations. What is repeated (tannim and net and feeding the beasts from 29, the sword of Babylon and 
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1492 \u1464 \u1502 \u1493 \u1465 \u1503 
\f1 \cf2  from 30, the 
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1491 \u1502 \u1492 
\f1 \cf2  question and the descent from 31) is repeated so that these new elements land on already-prepared ground.\
One place I'd invite pushback on myself: I've read vv. 13-14's oil-smooth waters as a genuine restoration note within a judgment oracle \'97 a clarified creation after the fouler is gone. But it could be read more bleakly, as simply the stillness of a depopulated land (no man, no beast to stir the water because all are dead). The Hebrew alone may underdetermine which. Does the 
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1496 \u1493 
\f1 \cf2  sequel \'97 
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1502 \u1464 \u1502 \u1464 \u1492 
\f1 \cf2  
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1493 \u1468 \u1504 \u1456 \u1513 \u1463 \u1473 \u1502 \u1464 \u1468 \u1492 
\f1 \cf2 , utter desolation \'97 pull v. 14 toward death-stillness rather than restoration? I lean toward death-stillness for Egypt specifically, with the clarified-water image functioning as the exiles' lesson rather than Egypt's comfort. But I'd want to test that against whether Ezekiel elsewhere lets a clarification image sit purely inside desolation without any forward turn.
