\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
I do think we have inclusio in this oracle. I am not entirely sure chiasm as such is fully present here, but the inclusio, the symmetric framing, extends even beyond the water. The outermost layer is the lament, the dirge. This is more than a genre notice, since the contents hardly follow the lament meter, nor do they stick entirely to the formula of emphasis on rise before fall.\
But if there is inclusio here, what is the rhetorical purpose of the framing? Similar to the lament framing of Ezekiel 19, or a similar technique used for a different purpose?\
As for the different moral register, while previous chapters do bring up Pharaoh's claim, they also bring up his effect on the shared medium. Before his fall, in being a false source of hope. After, in being a shock and a warning to the nations.\
And I think we have both registers in chapter 32 as well. If we read verse 2 as silenced, rather than you compare yourself or consider yourself, then we lose the self image as lion. More to the point, we lose the coherence of verse 3 as a response to a claim made in verse 2.\
There may still be a pun, setting up a claim as foreshadowing silencing of that claim. But for that to work, the primary meaning must be the claim.\
As for the new material, I would like more evidence before accepting that blood and darkness are being used with reference to the plagues. Blood certainly seems to have other rhetorical purposes in addition, if it is meant to refer to a plague. And darkness has been used in 31:15 to different effect as well. But Ezekiel has referred to Torah so many times in so many ways that the plagues are not improbable as a reference. But you already raised another possibility - Genesis.\
I agree that overall, we have the effect of declaring Pharaoh already dead. But I wonder if the rhetorical force of that declaration is to be found in the death coming to the nations yet living. Verse 9 speaks of troubling the nations, 10, terror. To be sure, they fear for their lives. But not because they will die, but because of the displayed power of the LORD, whom at least some will know as the LORD as a result. If there is an Exodus reference, it is in the terror of the nations at bringing down Egypt's military power - not an uncommon reference in earlier parts of the Hebrew Bible.\
I agree with your list of new elements, though that immediately brings up the question of what message these elements together send. On that matter, I note that all of them share something with the repeated word, nations.\
As for the oil-smooth waters, I do think verse 13 is clear enough. The cattle aren't muddying the waters because the LORD destroyed them. Other Egypt oracles have a note of hope. But if this is a note of hope, it is for others, those not killed, perhaps other nations no longer suffering Egypt's power projection or false hope. Or, more immediately in verse 15, those who now know.\
