\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
On the framing and its purpose. Ezekiel 19's 
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1511 \u1460 \u1497 \u1504 \u1464 \u1492 
\f1 \cf2  laments a monarchy that once had genuine glory (the lioness's cubs, the vine's strong branches) and grieves a real loss. The framing there works by pathos: the reader is drawn to mourn before recognizing complicity. Chapter 32's framing does something closer to the opposite. The dirge-form is held around contents that refuse the dirge's consolations \'97 no golden-age reminiscence, no "how are the mighty fallen" that lets glory stand. Instead the frame encloses churned mud, carrion-feeding, and a pit full of the uncircumcised. So the inclusio here is ironic in a way 19's is not: the genre promises to honor the dead, and the contents dishonor him at every turn (the tannim, not the lion; 
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1506 \u1458 \u1512 \u1461 \u1500 \u1460 \u1497 \u1501 
\f1 \cf2 , not 
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1490 \u1460 \u1468 \u1489 \u1468 \u1493 \u1465 \u1512 \u1460 \u1497 \u1501 
\f1 \cf2  at rest). The framing's purpose is to make the honoring-form itself the vehicle of humiliation \'97 you are given a state funeral, and the eulogy is your fouling of the water and your bed among the unclean. That is a different technique from 19, deployed for near-opposite effect: not to elicit grief but to withhold the very dignity the form pretends to offer.\
You've corrected me rightly on v. 2, and the correction is decisive. If v. 3's net (
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1493 \u1468 \u1508 \u1464 \u1512 \u1463 \u1513 \u1456 \u1474 \u1514 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 
\f1 \cf2  
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1506 \u1464 \u1500 \u1462 \u1497 \u1498 \u1464 
\f1 \cf2  
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1488 \u1462 \u1514 \u1470 \u1512 \u1460 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1514 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 
\f1 \cf2 ) is YHWH's response to Pharaoh's self-positioning as the water-beast that churns rivers, then the primary sense must be the claim \'97 "you consider yourself / you are like." The net catches a beast that thought itself sovereign in the water; it does not answer a mere announcement of silence. I withdraw 
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1504 \u1460 \u1491 \u1456 \u1502 \u1461 \u1497 \u1514 \u1464 
\f1 \cf2 -as-primary-silencing. The pun can only ride on top of the claim, as you say: the self-comparison stated so that the dirge-frame silences it. That actually strengthens the ironic-framing point \'97 the claim is voiced within a form whose business is to declare it over.\
And your point that both registers run through 29-31 and through 32 is well taken; I overdrew the contrast. The false-hope register (29:6-7's reed staff, 29:16's object of trust) and the shock-to-nations register (30's terror, 32:9-10) are both present throughout. What I'd now say is narrower and I think survives: the 
\i \cf2 water-fouling as a named crime against a shared medium
\i0 \cf2 , with its answering clarification, is the specific new content, sitting inside those two continuing registers rather than constituting a third.\
