\b \cf2 The date notice reframes the whole address \'97 and I think it resolves the Judah question against my earlier lean.
\b0 \cf2  Verse 1: twelfth year, twelfth month. Verse 17: twelfth year, fifteenth. Jerusalem fell in the eleventh year; the fugitive reaches the exiles in the twelfth year, tenth month (33:21). So this oracle is delivered to exiles who 
\i \cf2 already know the city is gone
\i0 \cf2 , or are within weeks of knowing. This is decisive. Egypt as object-of-trust (29:16) is no longer a live temptation to be preempted \'97 it is a 
\i \cf2 dead
\i0 \cf2  one to be interpreted. Egypt did not save Jerusalem. The reed staff (29:6-7) already broke in the exiles' hands, and their shoulders are already torn. So chapter 32 is not warning them off a trust they still hold; it is completing the funeral of a trust that has already failed them, and doing so by showing that the power they leaned on is itself now a corpse among corpses.\
And this cuts against the covenant-asymmetry comfort I offered last turn. Read after Jerusalem's fall, the parade of nations in the pit is not a company Judah is conspicuously 
\i \cf2 spared
\i0 \cf2  from \'97 it is the company Judah has just, visibly, joined in the relevant sense: her corporate agency, her Davidic 
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1492 \u1464 \u1502 \u1493 \u1465 \u1503 
\f1 \cf2 , her standing-in-the-land-of-the-living, is gone. The city fell. The oracle names Edom, Sidon, the princes of the north \'97 the immediate neighborhood into which Judah has been scattered and with which she now shares a fate of lost agency. The silence about Judah in the pit is therefore not exemption; it may be that Judah is not named because Judah is the 
\i \cf2 addressee
\i0 \cf2  \'97 the one being shown the pit, standing at its edge, recognizing her own corporate death in the death of Egypt and the company of the slain.\

\b \cf2 Which reopens the real question the sequel answers.
\b0 \cf2  If chapter 32, spoken post-fall, shows the exiles that corporate death has come to everyone they trusted and to themselves, then the thing that distinguishes Judah cannot be exemption from the pit. It can only be that the I who speaks \'97 the one figure in the chapter not given to death, who slays pride and displays it and stills the waters \'97 turns next, in chapter 33, to reopen the prophet's mouth toward 
\i \cf2 life
\i0 \cf2 . The nations in the pit have their identity and their shame and nothing more. Judah, equally dead as a corporate agent, is the one corpse the speaker addresses with a future. The asymmetry is not "spared the pit" but "addressed in it." That is a harder and I think truer reading than the one I gave: not that Judah escapes the company of the slain, but that the God who convenes the whole cosmos to witness Egypt's death is, in the very next breath, the God who asks the slain house of Israel why it will die when it could turn and live.\
So the larger message, assembled: YHWH convenes heaven, earth, and the nations to witness the killing of a 
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1490 \u1464 \u1468 \u1488 \u1493 \u1465 \u1503 
\f1 \cf2  \'97 the public falsification of autonomous, trusted power \'97 so that they know the water, the greatness, and the stilling were always His; and He does this for exiles who have just watched their own corporate greatness die, so that they learn which company is terminal (the pit, keeping only name and shame) and discover that their only non-terminal option is not any surviving greatness of their own but the sole speaker who was never given to death.
