That well wraps up our first pass at the message of that oracle.\
Now it is time to learn what the next and last of the Egypt oracles has to say. We have already been reaching into it for our discussion of the previous oracle, so we have already articulated some theories and questions about it, but we have yet to look into the details of the particular message the last oracle speaks, or the methods it uses to do so.\
It was appropriate to look forwards to this last oracle, as the last oracle is very much a comment on the previous oracle; it begins as the previous oracle ended, with the daughters of the nations mourning Egypt and its power as at a funeral.\
But there is also a very real sense in which the last oracle is a comment on the oracle with which chapter 31 ends. Lying in Sheol among the uncircumcised executed with the sword ends that earlier oracle, and is quickly picked up by this one.\
But with those similarities, we get changes, or at least elaboration on earlier messages. A wail, not a lament. Shame rather than honorable burial with one's weapons. Why?\
