Yes - armed even in death is exactly what Egypt does not get. As per chapter 30, both arms broken, unable to hold a sword.\
And since Ezekiel is speaking the LORD's words, that Ezekiel brings down Egypt is to say that the LORD brings down Egypt, as you said, on the LORD's terms. To the lowest, most shameful place.\
And we are told why - as with all but one of the other nations named, Egypt was a terror in the land of the living. Death stops that terror. Shameful death makes clear the LORD's position on that terror. A terror, somehow, even worse than the great heroes of old, or at least, so I read from the great heroes being given honorable burial despite their terror, and Egypt and Assyria and so on being given the opposite. So I think you are quite correct when you say that what these nations projected onto others they now carry as their own state.\
In addition to the images of terror, this oracle reframes in a second way the death of Egypt. As previous oracles did by emphasizing that the LORD was the one wielding the sword that was his agents, this oracle does by emphasizing the death of Egypt as an execution, presumably for the crime of terrorizing. The death of a criminal is shameful; but also, the execution highlights the judge who ordered it.\
And yes, seed and flowering is a good way to put that thing Ezekiel does; introduce an oracle in one oracle, grow it in another, so that we understand at last what we first saw.\
To answer the question of what we are to understand, we should introduce the other elements Ezekiel uses in this flowering. Verse 19, who do you surpass in beauty? The comfort, inverted from chapter 31, that Pharaoh takes. And the contrast in verse 32 between the living God and the buried in a death of shame Pharaoh and his army.\
